# One line ending in the repository, because two of them cost a campaign.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on this machine, so without this a fresh `.claude/worktrees/`
# checkout holds CRLF where the main checkout holds LF, for the same commit. Both
# fingerprints hash file bytes, so identical code fingerprinted differently in the two
# checkouts and a worktree measurement could never be delivered as current. The hashers
# now fold CRLF to LF before hashing, which repairs the number; `eol=lf` stops the drift
# that produced it. Both halves, because either alone leaves a trap for the other.
#
# Nothing tracked here is binary -- 831 files, none with a NUL byte in the first 8000 --
# so `text=auto` converts everything and guesses about nothing. The `-text` lines below
# name kinds that are not in the tree yet. `text=auto` would detect them on its own; the
# list is here so a first binary does not depend on a heuristic nobody is watching.
#
# Two directories override this and keep their own reasons. `deploy/` pins `* text eol=lf`
# because a shell script with CRLF is a syntax error on the box it runs on, and
# `assets/pieces/` pins `*.svg -text` because those are redistributed byte-for-byte and a
# translated checkout reads as a tampered file. Ten of those SVGs hold CRLF in the index,
# and `-text` is what keeps this file from touching them.
#
# Renormalisation: every tracked blob outside `assets/pieces/` is already LF, so
# `git add --renormalize` has nothing to do and no existing checkout goes dirty. A
# working tree that holds CRLF today still reads clean, because the clean filter folds it
# back to LF before git compares.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Passed through byte-for-byte.
*.png  -text
*.jpg  -text
*.jpeg -text
*.gif  -text
*.ico  -text
*.webp -text
*.woff  -text
*.woff2 -text
*.ttf  -text
*.otf  -text
*.pdf  -text
*.zip  -text
*.gz   -text
*.pyd  -text
*.dll  -text
*.so   -text
*.exe  -text
